# Swift Plans — Project Context

## Product

Swift Plans is a responsive web app for creating professional 2D floor plans for real-estate listings. A photographer opens it on an iPhone, enters room dimensions and relationships, and gets a clean plan for export (PNG/JPG/PDF/SVG) or a public interactive page. Geometry housekeeping (snapping, alignment, connected walls) is automatic and deterministic. Plans stay editable forever.

## Not in scope (do not build)

- Architectural CAD / construction documentation
- LiDAR, ARKit, RoomPlan, camera scanning, CV, photogrammetry, Matterport-style capture
- Any AI (room detection, geometry correction, labeling, measurement)
- Billing, subscriptions, Stripe, credits
- Organizations, teams, invitations, roles/permissions
- Offline mode / offline sync
- Native mobile apps (web only)
- CRM fields (agent, MLS number, price, brokerage, client)

If a task seems to require one of these, stop and say so.

## Core principles

1. **Speed over completeness** — fast, mostly-correct beats slow perfect; everything is editable later
2. **Simplicity over power** — prefer what makes the common case faster
3. **Mobile-first, desktop-equal** — same app, data model, and capabilities on both
4. **Structured data, not pictures** — plans are geometry; rendering/export derive from it
5. **Deterministic, not AI** — snapping/alignment use plain math
6. **Editable forever** — a plan opened months later is fully editable

## Architecture (do not re-litigate)

- **Stack:** Next.js App Router + TypeScript + React; Vercel; Supabase (Postgres, Auth, Storage)
- **Editor rendering:** custom SVG + React only — not Canvas, Konva, Fabric, PixiJS, or React Flow
- **Rooms are polygons:** created via width×depth if useful, but stored as vertices/walls from the start; no separate rectangle type
- **Storage:** relational rows for projects/floors/branding/publishing; one JSONB geometry document per floor
- **Units:** store inches only; feet/inches formatting is display-only (US units for now)
- **Performance:** drag/resize/snap against local state; debounced async autosave; never block interaction on a server round trip

## Development approach

- Do only what the current task asks; do not build ahead into future phases
- Do not refactor unrelated parts without being asked
- Preserve working functionality; warn before risky changes
- Warn before adding dependencies; prefer simple, readable solutions
- No auth UI, dashboard, or editor until their phases ask for them
